{"node_id": "real_073_Adaptation-in-a-heterogeneous-environment-II_13_HY_011", "node_type": "evidence", "status": "HYBRID_SHADOW_CANDIDATE", "unit_type": "TEXT", "source_atom_ids": ["D0012"], "source_fragment_ids": ["D0012_F001"], "source_visual_bundle_ids": [], "plain_text": "Here we consider a different approach: we fix the parameters $\\delta>0$ , $\\alpha>0$ , $\\mu>0$ and\nthe third optimum $\\mathbf{O}_3$ as $(\\mathbf{O}_1+\\mathbf{O}_2)/2$ . In other words, $\\mathbf{O}_3=(0,\\ldots,0)=\\mathcal{O}$ , with our\nhypothesis ( 19 ). We show that, when the two optima $\\mathbf{O}_1$ and $\\mathbf{O}_2$ are far enough from each\nother (meaning $\\beta>0$ large), this “ host in the middle ” configuration does not maximise\nthe difference $\\lambda_2(\\mathbf{O}_1,\\mathbf{O}_2) - \\lambda_3(\\mathbf{O}_1,\\mathbf{O}_2,\\mathbf{O}_3)$ , that is, it does not minimise $\\lambda_3(\\mathbf{O}_1,\\mathbf{O}_2,\\mathbf{O}_3)$ .\nThis result, consistent with the simulations in Figure 1 , is slightly surprising (at least at\nfirst glance) and shows the richness of the outcomes.", "semantic_text": "Here we consider a different approach: we fix the parameters $\\delta>0$ , $\\alpha>0$ , $\\mu>0$ and the third optimum $\\mathbf{O}_3$ as $(\\mathbf{O}_1+\\mathbf{O}_2)/2$ . In other words, $\\mathbf{O}_3=(0,\\ldots,0)=\\mathcal{O}$ , with our hypothesis ( 19 ). We show that, when the two optima $\\mathbf{O}_1$ and $\\mathbf{O}_2$ are far enough from each other (meaning $\\beta>0$ large), this “ host in the middle ” configuration does not maximise the difference $\\lambda_2(\\mathbf{O}_1,\\mathbf{O}_2) - \\lambda_3(\\mathbf{O}_1,\\mathbf{O}_2,\\mathbf{O}_3)$ , that is, it does not minimise $\\lambda_3(\\mathbf{O}_1,\\mathbf{O}_2,\\mathbf{O}_3)$ . This result, consistent with the simulations in Figure 1 , is slightly surprising (at least at first glance) and shows the richness of the outcomes.", "visual_description": null, "geometry_members": [{"member_id": "D0012_geometry", "bb